{"name":"Kimchi","hex":"#ed4b00","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"kimchi","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A Korean dish made of vegetables, such as cabbage or radishes, that are salted, seasoned, and stored in sealed containers to undergo lactic acid fermentation.","A Korean person."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kimchi"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kimchi","blocks":[{"text":"Kimchi is a traditional Korean side dish (banchan) consisting of salted and fermented vegetables, most often Chinese cabbage or Korean radish. A wide selection of seasonings are used, including gochugaru, spring onions, garlic, ginger, and jeotgal. Kimchi is also used in a variety of soups and stews. Kimchi is a staple food in Korean cuisine and is eaten as a side dish with almost every Korean meal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Various_kimchi.jpg/330px-Various_kimchi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kimchi","wikidata":"Q141898"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Kimchi_Day_Proclamation_Maryland_%282022-11-21%29.jpg/500px-Kimchi_Day_Proclamation_Maryland_%282022-11-21%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Kimchi_Day_Proclamation_Maryland_%282022-11-21%29.jpg/500px-Kimchi_Day_Proclamation_Maryland_%282022-11-21%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Proclamation signed by Governor of Maryland Larry Hogan declaring 22 November as 'Kimchi Day' (2022)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKimchi_Day_Proclamation_Maryland_(2022-11-21).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Making_kimchi.jpg/500px-Making_kimchi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Making_kimchi.jpg/500px-Making_kimchi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Basic ingredients for kimchi: napa cabbage, radish, carrot, salt, garlic, fish sauce, chili powder and scallions. A sticky, glutinous paste of rice flour is also needed to make the seasoning of the kimchi.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaking_kimchi.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Korean_cuisine-Salted_baechu-01.jpg/500px-Korean_cuisine-Salted_baechu-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Korean_cuisine-Salted_baechu-01.jpg/500px-Korean_cuisine-Salted_baechu-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Salted napa cabbage before making kimchi. Cabbage is usually marinated twice to help maintain the salt in the dish.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKorean_cuisine-Salted_baechu-01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Korean.Folk.Village-Minsokchon-02.jpg/500px-Korean.Folk.Village-Minsokchon-02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Korean.Folk.Village-Minsokchon-02.jpg/500px-Korean.Folk.Village-Minsokchon-02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Drying chili peppers for kimchi. After drying they are made into gochugaru, the powder that is added to the seasoning paste for spicy kimchi.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKorean.Folk.Village-Minsokchon-02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Korean.cuisine-Banchan-02.jpg/500px-Korean.cuisine-Banchan-02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Korean.cuisine-Banchan-02.jpg/1280px-Korean.cuisine-Banchan-02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Korean.cuisine-Banchan-02.jpg/500px-Korean.cuisine-Banchan-02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tongkimchi, gulgimchi (kimchi with additional oyster) and other banchan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKorean.cuisine-Banchan-02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Kimchi_jar.JPG/500px-Kimchi_jar.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Kimchi_jar.JPG/1280px-Kimchi_jar.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Kimchi_jar.JPG/500px-Kimchi_jar.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Empty traditional jars (onggi, 옹기), used for storing kimchi, gochujang, doenjang, soy sauce and other pickled banchan (side dishes)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKimchi_jar.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Korean.food-kimchi-02.jpg/500px-Korean.food-kimchi-02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Korean.food-kimchi-02.jpg/1280px-Korean.food-kimchi-02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Korean.food-kimchi-02.jpg/500px-Korean.food-kimchi-02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kimchi-buchimgae, a savory Korean pancake with kimchi","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKorean.food-kimchi-02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Korean-Dongchimi-01.jpg/500px-Korean-Dongchimi-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Korean-Dongchimi-01.jpg/1280px-Korean-Dongchimi-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Korean-Dongchimi-01.jpg/500px-Korean-Dongchimi-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dongchimi (동치미) is largely served during winter. Dongchimi is also used to make buckwheat naengmyeon, a popular dish during hot months.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKorean-Dongchimi-01.jpg"}],"sounds":{"kimchi":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/En-au-kimchi.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/b/b4/En-au-kimchi.ogg/En-au-kimchi.ogg.mp3"}]}}}